Testing a website and redirecting users to a maintenance page can be accomplished using a combination of scripting languages and web server configurations. Below, I'll provide a simple example using JavaScript (for client-side redirection) and a server-side script (using Node.js and Express for demonstration).

**Client-side Script (HTML + JavaScript):**

This script checks if the website is under maintenance and redirects users to a maintenance page if needed.

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>Website Maintenance</title>
</head>
<body>
    <h1>Website Maintenance</h1>
    <p>We're currently performing some maintenance. Please check back later.</p>

    <script>
        // Check if the website is under maintenance
        fetch('/api/maintenance')
            .then(response => response.json())
            .then(data => {
                if (data.maintenance) {
                    window.location.href = 'https://your-maintenance-page.com';
                }
            })
            .catch(error => console.error('Error checking maintenance status:', error));
    </script>
</body>
</html>
```

**Server-side Script (Node.js with Express):**

This server-side script will return a JSON response indicating whether the website is under maintenance.

```javascript
const express = require('express');
const app = express();

// Simulate maintenance mode
let isMaintenance = false;

app.get('/api/maintenance', (req, res) => {
    res.json({ maintenance: isMaintenance });
});

app.get('*', (req, res) => {
    if (isMaintenance) {
        res.redirect('https://your-maintenance-page.com');
    } else {
        res.sendFile(__dirname + '/index.html'); // Serve your regular HTML page
    }
});

const PORT = process.env.PORT || 3000;
app.listen(PORT, () => {
    console.log(`Server is running on port ${PORT}`);
});
